  content: `
  <p>You are sitting at the airport, watching the departure board change.</p>

  <p>First, the flight is delayed by 30 minutes. Then an hour. Then the word you really did not want to see appears: <strong>Cancelled</strong>.</p>

  <p>It is frustrating, but panicking rarely helps.</p>

  <p>The most important thing is to understand what has happened, find out what options you have, and act quickly before everyone else on the flight does the same.</p>

  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1436491865332-7a61a109cc05?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80" alt="Airplane waiting at an airport" class="in-article-img" />

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "A disrupted flight can change your itinerary. It does not have to ruin your entire trip."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>✈️ First: Check What Is Actually Happening</h2>

  <p>Before joining the longest line at the airport, confirm the latest information.</p>

  <p>Check the airline's app or website, airport departure screens, your booking email, and your flight status.</p>

  <p>Sometimes a delay shown on the airport board may change again. Other times, the airline may already have moved you to another flight before you speak to anyone.</p>

  <p>Having the latest information gives you a much better idea of what to do next.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>🔎 Check These First</h3>
    <p><strong>Flight status:</strong> Confirm whether your flight is delayed, cancelled, or waiting for further information.</p>
    <p><strong>New departure time:</strong> Check whether the airline has already provided an updated schedule.</p>
    <p><strong>Booking:</strong> Look for any automatic rebooking or itinerary changes.</p>
    <p><strong>Airport:</strong> Check the airport's own departure information as well.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>📱 1. Contact the Airline Before You Reach the Desk</h2>

  <p>If your flight has been cancelled, there is a good chance hundreds of other passengers are trying to speak to the same airline staff.</p>

  <p>Do not assume the airport desk is your only option.</p>

  <p>Try the airline's app, website, phone support, or online chat at the same time. If the airline has already offered alternative flights through its app, you may be able to resolve the problem without standing in line.</p>

  <p>If you do need to speak to an agent, be clear about what you need.</p>

  <p>Instead of simply asking, "What can you do?", ask which available options can get you to your destination as soon as reasonably possible.</p>

  <h2>🔄 2. Look at Your Rebooking Options</h2>

  <p>When an airline cancels a flight, it may offer an alternative itinerary.</p>

  <p>Check the new flight carefully before accepting it.</p>

  <p>Look at the departure time, arrival time, connection airport, number of stops, and whether the new itinerary still works with your plans.</p>

  <p>If you have a connecting flight, this becomes even more important.</p>

  <p>A replacement flight that arrives after your next connection leaves may solve one problem while creating another.</p>

  <h2>🛫 3. What If You Have a Connecting Flight?</h2>

  <p>Missed connections are one of the most stressful parts of flight disruptions.</p>

  <p>If your flights are on the same booking, contact the airline as soon as you know that your first flight will make you miss the connection.</p>

  <p>Do not wait until you arrive at the connecting airport if you already know there is a problem.</p>

  <p>If the flights were booked separately, the situation can be different. Your second airline may treat you as a no-show, so check its terms and contact it immediately if your first flight is disrupted.</p>

  <div class="destination-card">
    <h3>🧳 If You Are Going to Miss a Connection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Check whether both flights are on the same booking.</li>
      <li>Contact the airline as soon as possible.</li>
      <li>Ask about the earliest alternative route.</li>
      <li>Check whether your checked baggage will follow the new itinerary.</li>
      <li>Keep all updated booking confirmations.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>🏨 4. What About a Hotel You Already Paid For?</h2>

  <p>This is where travel insurance and booking policies can become important.</p>

  <p>If your flight disruption causes you to miss the first night of a hotel booking, contact the property as soon as you know you will be late.</p>

  <p>Explain the situation and ask whether they can modify the reservation.</p>

  <p>Do not assume you will automatically receive a refund. Hotel cancellation policies vary, particularly for non-refundable bookings.</p>

  <p>If you have travel insurance, keep records of the disruption and any additional expenses because your policy may have specific requirements for claims.</p>

  <h2>💳 5. Keep Your Receipts</h2>

  <p>A cancelled or severely delayed flight can create unexpected expenses.</p>

  <p>You may need food, transportation, accommodation, or other essentials while waiting for a new flight.</p>

  <p>Keep receipts for anything you spend because you may need them later for an airline request, insurance claim, or reimbursement process, depending on the circumstances and applicable rules.</p>

  <p>Also keep screenshots of your original flight, cancellation notices, new booking, and important messages from the airline.</p>

  <h2>⚖️ 6. Check What Rights Apply to Your Journey</h2>

  <p>Passenger rights are not identical everywhere.</p>

  <p>Depending on your route, airline, departure country, and the reason for the disruption, you may have rights relating to rebooking, refunds, meals, accommodation, or compensation.</p>

  <p>For example, flights covered by European passenger-rights rules can have specific protections for cancellations and long delays, while other journeys may be governed by different regulations.</p>

  <p>Do not assume that something you received on one trip automatically applies to another.</p>

  <p>Check the rules that apply to your specific journey and the airline's own conditions.</p>

  <h2>⏰ 7. If Your Flight Is Delayed, Don't Leave the Airport Too Quickly</h2>

  <p>A long delay can make you want to leave immediately.</p>

  <p>Sometimes that is perfectly reasonable. But before leaving, make sure you understand what the airline is doing with your booking.</p>

  <p>Flight times can change again, and you do not want to miss a newly assigned departure because you assumed the delay would last longer.</p>

  <p>If you leave the airport, keep checking the airline's app and notifications.</p>

  <h2>📲 8. Keep Checking Your Flight Status</h2>

  <p>Flight information can change quickly during disruption.</p>

  <p>Do not rely on a single notification from several hours earlier.</p>

  <p>Keep checking your booking and flight status, especially if you have been rebooked.</p>

  <p>FlySava can also be useful before and during a trip for checking flight options and keeping an eye on flight information, so you can understand your alternatives when your original plan changes.</p>

  <h2>🧠 9. Don't Accept the First Alternative Without Looking at It</h2>

  <p>The first replacement flight offered may not always be the best option for your trip.</p>

  <p>Depending on the airline and circumstances, there may be other routing possibilities.</p>

  <p>Ask whether there are alternative flights through another connection point or at a different time.</p>

  <p>But remember: the options available to you depend on your airline, ticket, route, and the rules governing the disruption.</p>

  <h2>🎒 10. What About Your Checked Baggage?</h2>

  <p>If your itinerary changes, do not assume your baggage situation automatically stays the same.</p>

  <p>Ask the airline whether your checked bag has been transferred to the new flight or whether you need to collect and recheck it.</p>

  <p>This is particularly important if you are rerouted through a different airport or staying overnight.</p>

  <p>Keep your baggage tag until you are reunited with your luggage.</p>

  <h2>🌍 What If the Airline Cancels Your Flight Before You Leave Home?</h2>

  <p>This is actually one of the easier situations to manage.</p>

  <p>You have more time, internet access, and usually a wider range of options than someone already stuck at the airport.</p>

  <p>Check your airline account first. You may already have been automatically rebooked.</p>

  <p>If the alternative does not work, contact the airline and review your options before heading to the airport.</p>

  <p>Also reconsider your hotel, airport transfer, car rental, and other bookings if your arrival time has changed significantly.</p>

  <h2>🛡️ How to Prepare Before Your Flight</h2>

  <p>The best time to prepare for a flight disruption is before one happens.</p>

  <p>Save your airline's app, keep your booking reference accessible, and make sure your phone is charged before leaving for the airport.</p>

  <p>If your trip is expensive or involves multiple non-refundable bookings, consider whether travel insurance makes sense for your circumstances.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>✈️ Before You Leave Home</h3>

    <p><strong>Save your booking:</strong> Keep your confirmation and booking reference easily accessible.</p>

    <p><strong>Download the airline app:</strong> Notifications can be faster than waiting at an airport desk.</p>

    <p><strong>Check your flight:</strong> Look at the latest status before leaving for the airport.</p>

    <p><strong>Keep essentials with you:</strong> Put medication, documents, chargers, and valuables in your cabin baggage.</p>

    <p><strong>Know your alternatives:</strong> If your route has multiple airlines or connections, understand the possible alternatives before your trip.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>❌ What Not to Do</h2>

  <p>When a flight is cancelled, frustration can lead to rushed decisions.</p>

  <p>Do not immediately book an expensive replacement flight without first checking what the original airline can offer.</p>

  <p>Do not throw away receipts.</p>

  <p>Do not assume that every expense will automatically be reimbursed.</p>

  <p>And do not rely entirely on social media posts or advice from another passenger. Your rights and options depend on the details of your specific journey.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What should I do first if my flight is cancelled?</summary>
    <p>Confirm the cancellation through the airline, check whether you have been automatically rebooked, and contact the airline to understand your available alternatives.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What happens if my flight is delayed?</summary>
    <p>Keep checking the latest flight information and follow the airline's instructions. If the delay affects a connection or another booking, contact the relevant provider as soon as possible.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Can I get compensation for a cancelled or delayed flight?</summary>
    <p>It depends on the route, airline, applicable passenger-rights rules, and reason for the disruption. Check the rules that apply to your specific journey rather than assuming compensation is automatic.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What happens if I miss my connecting flight?</summary>
    <p>If both flights are on the same booking, contact the airline as soon as possible because it may be able to arrange an alternative itinerary. Separate bookings can have different rules, so contact the second airline immediately as well.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Should I book another flight myself?</summary>
    <p>Not immediately. First check what the original airline can offer and understand your options. Booking a replacement independently may affect what you can later recover, depending on the circumstances.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

  <p>A delayed or cancelled flight is frustrating, but it does not have to become a travel disaster.</p>

  <p>Check the facts. Contact the airline quickly. Understand your alternatives. Protect your other bookings. Keep your receipts and records.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "When travel plans change, the smartest move is not to panic. It's to find the next option."
  </blockquote>

  <p>And before your next trip, make sure you know where to check your flight status and what your alternatives are.</p>

  <p><strong>Your original flight might not go according to plan. Your trip still can.</strong></p>
`
