  content: `
  <p>A hotel can look affordable when you first see the price.</p>

  <p>Then you add taxes, parking, breakfast, transportation, extra guest charges, and other costs—and suddenly the "cheap" hotel isn't so cheap anymore.</p>

  <p>Some hotel booking mistakes can also cost you in less obvious ways. A poor location can mean spending more on transportation. Choosing the wrong room can leave you uncomfortable. Booking a non-refundable rate without checking your plans can turn a small change into a much bigger expense.</p>

  <p>Before confirming a hotel, take a few minutes to look beyond the headline price.</p>

  <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/7820689/pexels-photo-7820689.jpeg" alt="Hotel room being prepared for travelers" class="in-article-img" />

  <h2>Booking Based Only on the Lowest Price</h2>

  <p>The cheapest room isn't always the cheapest option for your trip.</p>

  <p>A low-priced hotel might be far from the attractions you want to visit, have expensive parking, require taxis, or exclude features you'll need.</p>

  <p>Instead of comparing the nightly rate alone, calculate the likely total cost of staying there.</p>

  <p>A slightly more expensive hotel with a convenient location and fewer additional expenses may ultimately save you money.</p>

  <h2>Ignoring the Hotel's Location</h2>

  <p>Location is one of the biggest factors people underestimate when booking accommodation.</p>

  <p>A hotel can have an excellent room and a low price but still be inconvenient if it's far from everything on your itinerary.</p>

  <p>Before booking, check the distance to:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Your main attractions</li>
    <li>Public transportation</li>
    <li>Restaurants</li>
    <li>The airport or train station</li>
    <li>Business or event venues</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Then consider how you'll actually travel between these places.</p>

  <h2>Forgetting Transportation Costs</h2>

  <p>This mistake often happens when travelers compare hotels in different neighborhoods.</p>

  <p>A hotel that saves you money on the room can cost more if you need taxis every day.</p>

  <p>Even frequent public transportation journeys can add up over a longer stay.</p>

  <p>Estimate your likely transportation costs before deciding which hotel is the better deal.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>Calculate the Real Cost</h3>

    <p><strong>Hotel:</strong> Room rate + taxes + mandatory fees.</p>

    <p><strong>Transport:</strong> Airport transfer + daily travel.</p>

    <p><strong>Food:</strong> Breakfast and nearby meal costs.</p>

    <p><strong>Extras:</strong> Parking, resort fees, extra beds, or other services.</p>

    <p><strong>Total:</strong> What you'll realistically spend for the entire stay.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not Checking the Final Price</h2>

  <p>The first price you see isn't always the final amount you'll pay.</p>

  <p>Depending on the property and destination, additional charges can include taxes, mandatory fees, parking, resort or facility fees, cleaning charges, or extra guest costs.</p>

  <p>Always review the complete price before confirming your booking.</p>

  <p>Don't compare one hotel's initial price with another hotel's final price.</p>

  <h2>Choosing a Non-Refundable Rate Too Quickly</h2>

  <p>Non-refundable rates can be cheaper, but the savings come with less flexibility.</p>

  <p>If your plans change, you may not be able to recover the booking cost.</p>

  <p>Before choosing a non-refundable rate, check how certain your travel dates are.</p>

  <p>If there's a realistic chance your plans could change, compare the cost of a flexible rate with the amount you'd save by choosing the cheaper option.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "A cheaper booking isn't a better deal if changing your plans makes it expensive."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Assuming Every Flexible Rate Is Fully Flexible</h2>

  <p>The word "flexible" doesn't mean every booking has the same cancellation conditions.</p>

  <p>Check the exact deadline for cancellation and whether any deposit or portion of the booking is non-refundable.</p>

  <p>Also check whether changing your dates is allowed and whether the price difference will apply.</p>

  <p>Read the actual booking conditions before paying.</p>

  <h2>Booking the Wrong Room Category</h2>

  <p>Hotels can have several room categories that look almost identical in photos.</p>

  <p>But the differences can include room size, bed type, view, balcony, bathroom, occupancy, and included amenities.</p>

  <p>Make sure the room you select matches what you actually need.</p>

  <p>Don't assume that the hotel's best-looking photo represents every room category.</p>

  <h2>Not Checking Room Size</h2>

  <p>Hotel photography can make rooms appear larger than they feel in person.</p>

  <p>Check the listed room size before booking.</p>

  <p>This is especially important if you're traveling with family, staying for several nights, or carrying large luggage.</p>

  <p>A small price difference for a larger room can sometimes be worthwhile if you'll spend significant time there.</p>

  <h2>Paying for Features You Won't Use</h2>

  <p>It's easy to be attracted by hotels with long lists of facilities.</p>

  <p>But if you're spending most of your vacation exploring, paying extra for a spa, gym, multiple pools, or several restaurants may not make financial sense.</p>

  <p>Choose the facilities that actually matter to your trip.</p>

  <h2>Assuming Breakfast Is Always Worth It</h2>

  <p>Breakfast can be convenient, but it isn't automatically good value.</p>

  <p>Compare the hotel's breakfast cost with nearby cafés, restaurants, or supermarkets.</p>

  <p>Also consider how much your family or travel group is likely to eat.</p>

  <p>If you're leaving the hotel early every morning, paying for a large breakfast package may not make sense.</p>

  <h2>Ignoring Parking Costs</h2>

  <p>If you're driving, don't assume hotel parking is included.</p>

  <p>Check whether parking is:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Free or paid</li>
    <li>Available on-site</li>
    <li>Charged per night</li>
    <li>Limited in availability</li>
    <li>Subject to additional conditions</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A hotel with a lower room rate can become more expensive once several nights of parking are added.</p>

  <h2>Not Checking Public Transportation</h2>

  <p>If you're not driving, transportation access becomes even more important.</p>

  <p>Check whether the hotel is near a useful metro, train, tram, or bus connection.</p>

  <p>Don't stop at "station nearby." Check the actual routes and how many transfers you'll need to reach your main destinations.</p>

  <h2>Trusting Hotel Photos Too Much</h2>

  <p>Hotel photos are useful, but they don't tell you everything.</p>

  <p>Professional photography can make rooms look larger, quieter, brighter, or more luxurious than they feel during a normal stay.</p>

  <p>Compare official hotel photos with recent guest-uploaded images.</p>

  <p>Also read recent reviews for information about noise, cleanliness, maintenance, and room condition.</p>

  <h2>Skipping Recent Reviews</h2>

  <p>A hotel's reputation can change.</p>

  <p>Rooms may be renovated, management can change, nearby construction can begin, or service levels can improve or decline.</p>

  <p>Recent reviews are more useful for understanding what guests are experiencing now.</p>

  <p>Look for repeated patterns rather than focusing on one extreme review.</p>

  <h2>Ignoring the Neighborhood</h2>

  <p>A hotel can look excellent while its surrounding neighborhood doesn't fit your trip.</p>

  <p>Check what's around the property before booking.</p>

  <p>Look for restaurants, supermarkets, pharmacies, transportation, busy roads, nightlife areas, and anything else that could affect your stay.</p>

  <h2>Choosing a Hotel Too Far Away to Save Money</h2>

  <p>Going farther from the center can reduce the hotel rate.</p>

  <p>But there is a point where the savings stop being worthwhile.</p>

  <p>If you're spending an hour commuting every morning and evening, you're paying with your time as well as your money.</p>

  <p>Choose a location that balances accommodation cost with convenience.</p>

  <h2>Not Checking Check-In and Check-Out Times</h2>

  <p>Your flight schedule and hotel schedule don't always match.</p>

  <p>If you arrive early in the morning or late at night, check the hotel's check-in policy.</p>

  <p>If you have a late departure, check whether late check-out is available and whether it costs extra.</p>

  <p>Don't assume the hotel will automatically accommodate your preferred arrival or departure time.</p>

  <h2>Forgetting About Luggage Storage</h2>

  <p>If your arrival or departure doesn't line up with check-in and check-out, luggage storage can be useful.</p>

  <p>Check whether the hotel provides this service and whether any conditions or fees apply.</p>

  <p>This can save you from paying for an additional night simply because you need somewhere to leave your bags.</p>

  <h2>Booking Without Checking Occupancy Rules</h2>

  <p>Don't assume that a room's bed configuration tells you how many people can stay there.</p>

  <p>Check the property's maximum occupancy and policies for children, additional guests, and extra beds.</p>

  <p>This is particularly important for families and groups.</p>

  <h2>Forgetting About Extra Beds</h2>

  <p>A room may technically accommodate another guest but require an additional charge for an extra bed.</p>

  <p>Check whether the extra bed is included in the displayed price or added later.</p>

  <p>Also verify the type of sleeping arrangement before booking.</p>

  <h2>Not Considering Your Trip Length</h2>

  <p>The best hotel choice can change depending on how long you're staying.</p>

  <p>For a one-night stay, location and convenience may matter most.</p>

  <p>For a week-long stay, room size, laundry, kitchen access, breakfast options, and everyday neighborhood convenience may become more important.</p>

  <h2>Ignoring Food Options Around the Hotel</h2>

  <p>Accommodation is only one part of your daily travel budget.</p>

  <p>If you're staying somewhere with very expensive restaurants, your food costs can rise quickly.</p>

  <p>Look for supermarkets, cafés, local restaurants, and other affordable food options nearby.</p>

  <h2>Booking Without Comparing Alternatives</h2>

  <p>Finding a hotel that looks good doesn't mean you've found the best option.</p>

  <p>Compare several suitable properties before booking.</p>

  <p>Look at price, location, room size, reviews, amenities, cancellation conditions, and additional costs.</p>

  <p>A few minutes of comparison can prevent an expensive mistake.</p>

  <h2>Focusing Too Much on Star Ratings</h2>

  <p>Star ratings can be useful, but they don't tell you everything about the experience.</p>

  <p>A lower-star hotel in an excellent location can sometimes be more useful than a higher-rated hotel far away from your itinerary.</p>

  <p>Look at the actual facilities, recent reviews, room quality, and location instead of relying on the star rating alone.</p>

  <h2>Ignoring the Hotel's Cancellation Deadline</h2>

  <p>Even when you choose a refundable booking, there is usually a specific deadline for cancelling without penalty.</p>

  <p>Put that date somewhere you'll remember it.</p>

  <p>Missing the deadline can turn a flexible reservation into an expensive one.</p>

  <h2>Not Checking Currency and Payment Details</h2>

  <p>When booking internationally, pay attention to the currency displayed and the currency you'll actually be charged in.</p>

  <p>Also check whether payment is required immediately or at the property.</p>

  <p>Understanding the payment terms can help you avoid surprises when the booking is charged.</p>

  <h2>Booking a Great Hotel in the Wrong Location</h2>

  <p>This deserves repeating because it's one of the most expensive mistakes in terms of both money and time.</p>

  <p>You can book an excellent hotel and still have a disappointing stay if the location doesn't match your plans.</p>

  <p>Before booking, place the hotel on your itinerary and see how much daily travel it creates.</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>Before You Click "Book"</h3>

    <p><strong>Price:</strong> Have you checked the final total?</p>

    <p><strong>Location:</strong> Does the hotel fit your itinerary?</p>

    <p><strong>Transport:</strong> What will getting around cost?</p>

    <p><strong>Room:</strong> Are the size, bed, and occupancy suitable?</p>

    <p><strong>Reviews:</strong> What are recent guests saying?</p>

    <p><strong>Policies:</strong> Have you checked cancellation and check-in terms?</p>

    <p><strong>Extras:</strong> Are parking, breakfast, or other services charged separately?</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Compare the Hotel as Part of the Whole Trip</h2>

  <p>The smartest hotel booking isn't necessarily the one with the lowest room price.</p>

  <p>It's the one that works well with your itinerary and doesn't create unnecessary expenses elsewhere.</p>

  <p>Consider accommodation, transportation, food, facilities, flexibility, and your own comfort together.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "The real cost of a hotel is more than the number shown beside the room."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Compare Hotels Carefully With FlySava</h2>

  <p>When comparing hotels on FlySava, look beyond the headline price.</p>

  <p>Compare the location, room type, amenities, reviews, cancellation terms, additional charges, and transportation access before making your final decision.</p>

  <p>A few extra minutes of research can help you avoid costs that become obvious only after you've booked.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What is the biggest hotel booking mistake?</summary>
    <p>One of the biggest mistakes is choosing a hotel based only on the lowest room price without considering location, transportation, additional fees, and the actual room you'll receive.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>How can I avoid hidden hotel fees?</summary>
    <p>Review the complete booking price and check the property's policies for taxes, mandatory fees, parking, breakfast, extra guests, resort or facility fees, and other charges.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Should I always choose a refundable hotel booking?</summary>
    <p>Not necessarily. Compare the price difference and consider how likely your plans are to change. A refundable rate can be valuable when flexibility matters.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Why is hotel location so important?</summary>
    <p>A convenient location can reduce transportation costs and travel time. A cheaper hotel in an inconvenient area can cost more overall once transportation is included.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Should I trust hotel photos?</summary>
    <p>Use hotel photos as a starting point, but also check recent guest photos and reviews. Professional photos may not show the property's everyday condition or atmosphere.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Should I compare hotels by star rating?</summary>
    <p>Star ratings can provide useful context, but they shouldn't be your only comparison. Location, room quality, reviews, amenities, and total cost are also important.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Can a more expensive hotel actually save money?</summary>
    <p>Yes. A hotel with a better location, included breakfast, free parking, or fewer additional charges can sometimes cost less overall than a cheaper hotel with more expenses.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>Most expensive hotel mistakes happen because travelers focus on the room price and overlook everything around it.</p>

  <p>Check the location, calculate transportation costs, understand the final price, choose the right room, read recent reviews, and carefully review cancellation and payment terms.</p>

  <p><strong>Before booking a hotel, don't ask only "How much is the room?" Ask "How much will this stay actually cost me?"</strong></p>
`
