  content: `
    <p>Few things capture the history of the United Kingdom quite like its castles. Some were built as military fortresses, others became royal residences, while many now stand as fascinating reminders of Britain's medieval and royal past.</p>

    <p>From grand royal castles near major cities to dramatic ruins surrounded by countryside, there are hundreds of historic sites to discover across England, Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland.</p>

    <h2>Why Visit UK Castles?</h2>

    <p>Castles offer more than impressive architecture. They can reveal stories about medieval battles, royal families, changing borders, military technology, and everyday life throughout different periods of British history.</p>

    <p>Many are also located in beautiful landscapes, making them worthwhile stops even for travelers who are not particularly interested in history.</p>

    <h2>1. Edinburgh Castle, Scotland</h2>

    <p>Edinburgh Castle is one of Scotland's most recognizable landmarks. Sitting on Castle Rock above Edinburgh's Old Town, it dominates the city's skyline.</p>

    <p>The castle has played an important role in Scottish history and offers views across the city. It is a natural addition to any first visit to Edinburgh.</p>

    <p><a href="/blog/edinburgh-travel-guide" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Edinburgh Travel Guide: Things to Do & Places to Visit &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>2. Windsor Castle, England</h2>

    <p>Windsor Castle is one of the world's best-known royal residences and has a long connection with the British monarchy.</p>

    <p>Located in Windsor, west of London, it can also work as part of a wider trip around southern England.</p>

    <h2>3. Warwick Castle, England</h2>

    <p>Warwick Castle has a long history dating back to the medieval period. Today, visitors can explore the castle grounds, towers, interiors, and surrounding attractions.</p>

    <p>Its location also makes it possible to combine a castle visit with exploring the historic town of Warwick.</p>

    <h2>4. Tower of London, England</h2>

    <p>The Tower of London is more fortress than traditional fairytale castle, but its history makes it one of the UK's most important historic attractions.</p>

    <p>Located beside the River Thames, the complex has served different purposes over the centuries, including royal residence, fortress, and prison.</p>

    <h2>5. Caernarfon Castle, Wales</h2>

    <p>Wales is home to some of Britain's most dramatic castles, and Caernarfon Castle is one of the best known.</p>

    <p>Its imposing walls and towers reflect the military importance of castles during the medieval period. It is particularly interesting for travelers exploring northern Wales.</p>

    <h2>6. Conwy Castle, Wales</h2>

    <p>Conwy Castle is another spectacular Welsh fortress. Its towers and defensive walls overlook the town and surrounding landscape.</p>

    <p>The castle can be combined with a visit to Conwy itself, making it a useful stop on a wider North Wales itinerary.</p>

    <h2>7. Stirling Castle, Scotland</h2>

    <p>Stirling Castle is closely connected with Scottish royal history and occupies an important position overlooking the surrounding landscape.</p>

    <p>It is a useful addition for travelers interested in Scotland's history beyond Edinburgh.</p>

    <h2>Castles in England, Scotland or Wales?</h2>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>🏰 England</h3>
      <p>England offers royal residences, medieval fortresses, historic towers, and castles close to major cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>🏴 Scotland</h3>
      <p>Scottish castles range from major royal strongholds to atmospheric ruins surrounded by mountains and countryside.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>🐉 Wales</h3>
      <p>Wales is particularly famous for its medieval castles, many of which are dramatic stone fortresses surrounded by beautiful landscapes.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How to Plan a UK Castle Trip</h2>

    <p>You do not need to visit dozens of castles to experience Britain's history. Choose a few based on your route and interests.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Combine castles with nearby towns and attractions.</li>
      <li>Check opening times before traveling.</li>
      <li>Allow extra time for large castle complexes.</li>
      <li>Wear comfortable shoes for stairs, courtyards, and uneven ground.</li>
      <li>Check weather conditions if the castle involves outdoor walking.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="flight-tips-card">
      <h3>🗺️ Planning Tip</h3>
      <p>Don't choose castles randomly from a map. Build them into your existing route so you spend less time traveling between attractions.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Best Time to Visit UK Castles</h2>

    <p>Castles can be visited throughout the year, but outdoor castle grounds are generally easier to enjoy when daylight hours are longer and weather conditions are comfortable.</p>

    <p>Spring and summer can work well for combining castles with countryside walks, while autumn offers a different atmosphere and seasonal scenery.</p>

    <p><a href="/blog/best-time-to-visit-united-kingdom" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Best Time to Visit the UK: Weather, Seasons & Travel Tips &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Which UK countries have the most famous castles?</summary>
      <p>England, Scotland, and Wales all have major historic castles. Wales is particularly well known for its concentration of medieval fortresses, while Scotland has many castles associated with royal and clan history.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Can I visit UK castles without a car?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Several major castles can be reached using public transportation, although a car can provide more flexibility when exploring rural areas.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Are UK castles open all year?</summary>
      <p>Opening dates and hours vary by castle. Always check the individual attraction before planning your visit.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What should I wear when visiting castles?</summary>
      <p>Comfortable walking shoes and weather-appropriate layers are useful because many castle visits involve outdoor areas, stairs, and uneven surfaces.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>UK castles are an easy way to add history and scenery to a British holiday. From Edinburgh and Windsor to the dramatic fortresses of Wales, each castle offers a different glimpse into the country's past.</p>

    <p>Rather than trying to visit as many as possible, choose castles that naturally fit your itinerary and give yourself enough time to explore them properly.</p>
  `
