  content: `
    <p>Mexico is often presented through images of turquoise water, palm-lined beaches and all-inclusive resorts. Those places are certainly part of the country, but they only tell a small part of the story.</p>

    <p>Beyond the coast, Mexico has enormous cities, colorful colonial towns, archaeological sites, mountain landscapes, traditional markets and food cultures that can vary dramatically from one region to another.</p>

    <p>If you want your trip to feel like more than a beach holiday, there are plenty of ways to experience Mexico differently.</p>

    <h2>Start With Mexico City</h2>

    <p>Mexico City is one of the easiest places to understand how modern Mexico and its history exist side by side.</p>

    <p>The historic centre is filled with grand buildings, plazas and museums, while neighborhoods such as Roma and Condesa offer cafés, restaurants, galleries and a more contemporary atmosphere.</p>

    <p>One of the city's biggest advantages for travelers is variety. You can spend the morning exploring museums, have lunch at a local market and finish the afternoon walking through a leafy neighborhood.</p>

    <p>Since you already have our <a href="/blog/mexico-city-has-too-much-to-see-heres-how-to-choose" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Mexico City guide &rarr;</a>, use it to decide which parts of the city fit your travel style.</p>

    <h2>Discover Mexico's Ancient Past</h2>

    <p>Mexico's archaeological sites are another reason to travel beyond the beaches. Across the country, ancient civilizations left behind cities, temples and ceremonial centres that provide a completely different travel experience.</p>

    <p>Chichén Itzá in the Yucatán is one of the country's best-known archaeological sites, while Teotihuacán near Mexico City is famous for its enormous pyramids and ancient urban landscape.</p>

    <p>Other sites, including Palenque in Chiapas and Monte Albán near Oaxaca, offer different settings and histories. Some are surrounded by forests or mountains, making the journey itself part of the experience.</p>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>Ancient Mexico Worth Exploring</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Teotihuacán:</strong> A major archaeological site near Mexico City.</li>
        <li><strong>Chichén Itzá:</strong> One of the Yucatán Peninsula's most famous ancient sites.</li>
        <li><strong>Palenque:</strong> Known for its impressive setting in Chiapas.</li>
        <li><strong>Monte Albán:</strong> An important archaeological site overlooking Oaxaca.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Spend Time in Oaxaca</h2>

    <p>Oaxaca is a strong choice if you want to combine culture, food, architecture and traditional crafts in one trip.</p>

    <p>The city has colorful streets, markets, historic buildings and a food culture that deserves time rather than a quick stop. Outside the city, you can also explore archaeological sites, mountain communities and craft villages.</p>

    <p>Food is another major reason to include Oaxaca in your itinerary. Mole, tlayudas, tamales and regional drinks are part of the local experience rather than simply things to tick off a menu.</p>

    <p>If food is one of your reasons for visiting Mexico, our <a href="/blog/what-to-eat-on-your-first-trip-to-mexico" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Mexico food guide &rarr;</a> is a useful starting point.</p>

    <h2>Explore Mexico's Colonial Towns</h2>

    <p>Mexico's historic towns offer another perspective. Places such as San Miguel de Allende, Guanajuato and Puebla are known for colorful architecture, plazas, churches and walkable historic areas.</p>

    <p>These destinations can be particularly enjoyable if you prefer slower travel. Instead of spending the entire day moving between attractions, you can explore on foot, stop for coffee, visit a market and enjoy the atmosphere of the city.</p>

    <p>Each destination has its own character, so it is worth choosing based on what you want from the trip rather than trying to visit too many places in one journey.</p>

    <h2>Mexico Is Also About Everyday Culture</h2>

    <p>One of the best ways to experience Mexico is to pay attention to everyday life. Visit a local market, try regional food, walk through a neighborhood or spend time in a public plaza.</p>

    <p>You do not need an expensive tour for every part of your trip. Some of the most memorable moments can come from simple experiences that allow you to observe how a place feels.</p>

    <div class="travel-tips-card">
      <h3>Travel Beyond the Checklist</h3>
      <p>Choose a few major attractions, then leave space for markets, cafés, neighborhood walks and spontaneous discoveries. Mexico becomes much more interesting when you give yourself time to experience it rather than simply photograph it.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How to Plan a Mexico Trip Beyond the Beaches</h2>

    <p>Mexico is large, so trying to combine Mexico City, Oaxaca, the Yucatán, Chiapas and the Pacific coast in one short holiday can leave you spending more time in transit than exploring.</p>

    <p>Instead, choose one region and build around it. A first trip could combine Mexico City with nearby destinations, while another itinerary could focus on Oaxaca and southern Mexico.</p>

    <p>When comparing flights, consider which airport makes the most sense for your route. You may find that flying into one city and departing from another works better than returning to your original starting point.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is Mexico only good for beach holidays?</summary>
      <p>No. Mexico has major cities, archaeological sites, colonial towns, mountain regions, markets and diverse food traditions in addition to its beaches.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What is a good cultural destination in Mexico?</summary>
      <p>Mexico City and Oaxaca are both strong choices, with museums, historic areas, markets, food and cultural experiences.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Which ancient ruins should I visit in Mexico?</summary>
      <p>Teotihuacán, Chichén Itzá, Palenque and Monte Albán are among the major archaeological sites travelers can consider.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>How many places should I visit on a Mexico trip?</summary>
      <p>It depends on the length of your trip, but focusing on one or two regions is often more comfortable than trying to cross the entire country.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Mexico's beaches may be what first attracts many travelers, but the country's cities, ruins, food and local traditions can turn a simple holiday into a much richer experience.</p>

    <p>Whether you explore Mexico City, walk through Oaxaca, visit ancient ruins or spend an afternoon in a historic town, there is plenty to discover away from the coast.</p>
  `
