  content: `
    <p>Tacos may be the first food most people associate with Mexico, and there is a good reason for that. But Mexican cuisine is far more diverse than one dish.</p>

    <p>Different regions have their own ingredients, cooking methods and specialties. A trip through Mexico can take you from slow-cooked stews and rich sauces to fresh seafood, market breakfasts and sweet treats.</p>

    <p>If your Mexico trip is going to include plenty of food stops, here are some dishes worth looking beyond tacos for.</p>

    <h2>Start With Chilaquiles</h2>

    <p>Chilaquiles are a popular Mexican breakfast made with tortilla pieces cooked with salsa and commonly topped with ingredients such as cheese, onion, cream and eggs.</p>

    <p>They are especially useful for travelers who want to try something filling in the morning before a day of sightseeing.</p>

    <p>The salsa can vary, which means the same dish can taste quite different depending on where you eat it. Try it at a local breakfast spot rather than assuming every version will be the same.</p>

    <h2>Try Mole in Oaxaca</h2>

    <p>Mole is one of Mexico's most famous examples of complex regional cooking. It is not simply one sauce but a broad family of preparations with different ingredients and styles.</p>

    <p>Oaxaca is particularly associated with several varieties of mole. Some versions combine chiles, spices, nuts, seeds and other ingredients to create deep, layered flavors.</p>

    <p>If you are visiting Oaxaca, give yourself enough time to explore its food culture rather than treating the city as a quick stop.</p>

    <div class="food-card">
      <h3>Food Stops Worth Adding</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chilaquiles:</strong> A classic breakfast choice.</li>
        <li><strong>Mole:</strong> A rich family of regional sauces.</li>
        <li><strong>Pozole:</strong> A hearty soup or stew made with hominy and meat.</li>
        <li><strong>Tamales:</strong> Masa-based parcels prepared with different fillings.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Give Pozole a Try</h2>

    <p>Pozole is a comforting dish built around hominy, broth and meat, with toppings that can include lettuce or cabbage, onion, radish, lime and chili.</p>

    <p>There are different regional versions, so do not expect every bowl to taste identical. It can be especially satisfying after a long day of exploring.</p>

    <h2>Don't Skip Tamales</h2>

    <p>Tamales are made from masa and cooked in a wrapper, commonly with fillings such as meat, cheese, vegetables or sauces. They are found across Mexico and are an important part of everyday and celebratory food traditions.</p>

    <p>Because preparation varies by region, trying tamales in different destinations can become its own small food adventure.</p>

    <h2>Try Cochinita Pibil in the Yucatán</h2>

    <p>If your trip takes you to the Yucatán Peninsula, look for cochinita pibil. This regional specialty is traditionally associated with slow-cooked pork prepared with local seasonings.</p>

    <p>It is often served in tacos or other preparations, but the dish itself has a distinct regional identity.</p>

    <p>The Yucatán is also a good example of why Mexican cuisine cannot be reduced to one national menu. Ingredients and cooking traditions change significantly from region to region.</p>

    <h2>Mexican Street Food Is Part of the Experience</h2>

    <p>Markets and street food stalls can be some of the best places to understand local eating habits.</p>

    <p>You might find quesadillas, elotes, esquites, tortas, tamales or other regional snacks depending on where you are. Instead of searching for a single “best” dish, pay attention to what people around you are actually ordering.</p>

    <div class="food-card">
      <h3>Simple Street-Food Approach</h3>
      <p>Choose busy stalls, watch how the food is prepared, start with a small portion and try different dishes instead of ordering one huge meal. It is an easy way to discover more flavors.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Save Room for Dessert</h2>

    <p>Mexican food is not only about savory dishes. Churros, flan, pan dulce and other sweets can easily become part of your daily food stops.</p>

    <p>Try traditional bakeries in the morning or pick up a sweet snack while exploring a neighborhood. Mexican desserts often provide a nice contrast to spicy or savory meals.</p>

    <p>For a broader introduction to eating in Mexico, read our <a href="/blog/what-to-eat-on-your-first-trip-to-mexico" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">first-trip Mexico food guide &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <h2>Let the Region Choose the Menu</h2>

    <p>One of the easiest ways to make your food experience better is to stop looking for one universal list of Mexican dishes.</p>

    <p>In Oaxaca, explore moles and local specialties. In the Yucatán, look for regional dishes such as cochinita pibil. In coastal destinations, seafood can play a much bigger role. In Mexico City, markets and restaurants can introduce you to flavors from many parts of the country.</p>

    <p>Food becomes more interesting when it reflects where you are.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What Mexican food should I try besides tacos?</summary>
      <p>Chilaquiles, mole, pozole, tamales, cochinita pibil, enchiladas and regional street foods are all worth trying.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What Mexican food is popular for breakfast?</summary>
      <p>Chilaquiles are a popular choice, while tamales and other regional breakfast dishes can also be found depending on the destination.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Where should I try mole?</summary>
      <p>Oaxaca is particularly well known for its mole traditions, with different varieties offering very different flavors.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is Mexican food the same throughout the country?</summary>
      <p>No. Mexican cuisine varies significantly by region, with different ingredients, cooking techniques and specialties.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Tacos may introduce you to Mexican cuisine, but they should not be where your food journey ends.</p>

    <p>Try breakfast dishes, regional sauces, slow-cooked meals, market snacks and desserts. Most importantly, let the destination influence what you eat. That is often when a Mexico trip becomes a genuine food experience.</p>
  `
