  content: `
    <p>Canada is far too large to experience in one week, so the best approach is to focus on one region. This seven-day itinerary combines Vancouver with the Canadian Rockies and a scenic road trip, giving you a mix of city life, mountains, lakes and outdoor experiences.</p>

    <p>You will not see everything Canada has to offer, but you will get a taste of why western Canada attracts travelers from around the world.</p>

    <h2>Day 1 — Arrive in Vancouver</h2>

    <p>Start your trip in Vancouver and keep the first day relatively relaxed, especially if you are arriving after a long international flight.</p>

    <p>Spend the afternoon exploring the waterfront or walking around downtown. If you still have energy, head toward Stanley Park for a short walk along the seawall.</p>

    <p>Do not try to fit every attraction into your first few hours. Vancouver is a city that is enjoyable when you simply walk, stop for coffee and take in the views.</p>

    <p>Our <a href="/blog/vancouver-beyond-the-skyline-mountains-markets-and-the-coast" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Vancouver guide &rarr;</a> has more ideas for exploring the city.</p>

    <h2>Day 2 — Vancouver's City and Coast</h2>

    <p>Use your second day to experience more of Vancouver before beginning your road trip.</p>

    <p>Start with Stanley Park, then continue toward Granville Island for food and local shopping. Spend some time around the waterfront before exploring Gastown or another neighborhood.</p>

    <p>If the weather is good, finish the day near English Bay or Kitsilano. The combination of ocean, mountains and city skyline is one of Vancouver's defining experiences.</p>

    <div class="itinerary-card">
      <h3>Vancouver Day 2</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Stanley Park</li>
        <li>Granville Island</li>
        <li>Gastown</li>
        <li>Waterfront walk</li>
        <li>English Bay or Kitsilano</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Day 3 — Begin the Road Trip</h2>

    <p>Pick up your rental car and start heading toward the Canadian Rockies. The journey is part of the experience, so avoid planning too many activities for this day.</p>

    <p>Depending on your route and pace, you can stop at viewpoints, small communities or scenic areas along the way.</p>

    <p>Break the journey with regular stops instead of trying to reach your destination as quickly as possible. A road trip through western Canada is as much about the landscape between destinations as the final stop.</p>

    <h2>Day 4 — Discover Banff</h2>

    <p>Spend your fourth day exploring Banff National Park. This is where the itinerary shifts from city experiences to mountain scenery.</p>

    <p>Choose a few sights rather than trying to visit every lake and trail in one day. Lake Louise and Moraine Lake are among the best-known places in the region, while Banff town itself offers restaurants, shops and easy access to surrounding scenery.</p>

    <p>If hiking is part of your plan, choose a trail that matches your experience and current conditions.</p>

    <p>For more detailed planning, see our <a href="/blog/banff-national-park-travel-guide-lakes-hikes-wildlife" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Banff National Park travel guide &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <h2>Day 5 — Lakes, Mountains and Open Roads</h2>

    <p>Use another day around the Rockies rather than immediately moving to another destination.</p>

    <p>Spend the morning visiting a scenic lake or taking a short hike. In the afternoon, drive along one of the area's scenic routes and stop at viewpoints when conditions allow.</p>

    <p>Keep your schedule flexible. Mountain weather can change quickly, and some outdoor activities may take longer than expected.</p>

    <div class="travel-tips-card">
      <h3>Road Trip Tip</h3>
      <p>Do not plan every hour of a mountain road trip. Leave room for viewpoints, weather changes, wildlife sightings and unexpected stops.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Day 6 — A Final Rocky Mountain Morning</h2>

    <p>Spend your final full day enjoying the Rockies before beginning the journey back or preparing for your departure.</p>

    <p>You could take a relaxed morning walk around Banff, visit another viewpoint or simply enjoy breakfast with mountain views.</p>

    <p>If your route allows, use the afternoon for part of your return drive. Breaking the journey into manageable sections is better than attempting a very long drive late in the day.</p>

    <h2>Day 7 — Return and Depart</h2>

    <p>Use your final morning according to your departure plans. If you are flying home from Calgary, leave enough time for the drive from the Rockies to the airport, rental-car return and airport procedures.</p>

    <p>If your return flight is later, you can use the morning for one final walk or breakfast before leaving the region.</p>

    <h2>Why This Route Works</h2>

    <p>This itinerary keeps the trip focused on western Canada instead of trying to combine Vancouver with Toronto, Montreal or the Atlantic provinces.</p>

    <p>You get two very different experiences: a Pacific coastal city and the mountains of Alberta and British Columbia. The road trip then becomes the connection between them.</p>

    <p>Canada is enormous, so limiting the number of regions you visit is often the best way to make a short trip feel less rushed.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Can I visit Vancouver and Banff in seven days?</summary>
      <p>Yes. A focused itinerary can combine a few days in Vancouver with several days in the Canadian Rockies, although you should allow enough time for the long-distance travel between them.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Do I need a car for this Canada itinerary?</summary>
      <p>A rental car can be useful for the road-trip portion and for exploring areas around Banff. You may not need one for your time in central Vancouver.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is seven days enough for Canada?</summary>
      <p>Seven days is enough for a focused regional trip, but not for seeing the entire country. Choosing western Canada keeps the itinerary more realistic.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>When should I visit the Canadian Rockies?</summary>
      <p>The best timing depends on the activities you want to do and the conditions at the time of travel. Check current weather, road and trail conditions before planning outdoor activities.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>A seven-day trip to Canada works best when you stop trying to see all of Canada. Vancouver and the Canadian Rockies offer enough variety for an entire week, with the road between them becoming part of the adventure.</p>

    <p>Keep your route focused, leave room for the unexpected and give yourself enough time to enjoy the landscapes rather than simply driving past them.</p>
  `
