  content: `
    <p>Nepal can look surprisingly simple on a map. You choose Kathmandu, add Pokhara or a trek, and then start planning.</p>

    <p>In reality, Nepal's geography, altitude, weather and transportation can make a trip feel very different from what you expect.</p>

    <p>First-time visitors do not need to know everything before arriving, but avoiding a few common assumptions can make the journey much easier.</p>

    <h2>Trying to See Too Much Too Quickly</h2>

    <p>One of the easiest mistakes is building an itinerary around every place you have heard about.</p>

    <p>Nepal may not look huge compared with some countries, but mountain geography can make journeys slower than expected.</p>

    <p>Instead of constantly changing hotels, choose a few places and give yourself enough time to experience them properly.</p>

    <div class="travel-tips-card">
      <h3>A More Comfortable Approach</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Choose fewer destinations.</li>
        <li>Leave space between major travel days.</li>
        <li>Allow extra time for delays.</li>
        <li>Do not schedule demanding activities immediately after a long journey.</li>
        <li>Keep at least some parts of the itinerary flexible.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Underestimating Altitude</h2>

    <p>Altitude is one of the biggest things to consider if your trip includes high-altitude areas.</p>

    <p>Traveling from a low elevation to places such as Kathmandu or higher mountain destinations can require an adjustment period.</p>

    <p>Do not assume that being physically active at home automatically means you will immediately feel comfortable at altitude.</p>

    <p>If you have concerns about altitude or have relevant medical conditions, speak with a healthcare professional before your trip.</p>

    <h2>Assuming Nepal Has One Type of Weather</h2>

    <p>Nepal's geography creates significant differences in climate and conditions between regions and elevations.</p>

    <p>A warm day in one destination does not necessarily tell you what conditions will be like somewhere higher in the mountains.</p>

    <p>This is why checking the weather for each stop on your route is more useful than looking at one general Nepal forecast.</p>

    <h2>Packing Only for the Mountains</h2>

    <p>If you are not trekking, you may assume you can pack very casually. If you are trekking, you may assume you need only heavy outdoor clothing.</p>

    <p>Both approaches can create problems.</p>

    <p>Layering is generally more useful because temperatures can change throughout the day and between destinations.</p>

    <p>Comfortable walking shoes, sun protection and a light outer layer can be useful even on a city-focused itinerary.</p>

    <h2>Thinking Nepal Is Only About Trekking</h2>

    <p>Trekking is one of Nepal's major travel experiences, but it is not the only reason to visit.</p>

    <p>Kathmandu Valley offers historic cities and traditional architecture, while Pokhara provides lakeside scenery and mountain views without requiring a major trek.</p>

    <p>There are also markets, food experiences, cultural sites and countryside landscapes.</p>

    <p>Read <a href="/blog/the-nepal-you-find-between-the-mountains" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">The Nepal You Find Between the Mountains &rarr;</a> for more ideas.</p>

    <h2>Only Eating the Foods You Already Know</h2>

    <p>It is easy to stick to familiar international meals when traveling somewhere new.</p>

    <p>But Nepalese food is part of the experience.</p>

    <p>Try momos, dal bhat, Newari dishes and regional foods rather than treating meals as something separate from sightseeing.</p>

    <p>Our <a href="/blog/a-taste-of-nepal-from-momos-to-dal-bhat" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">A Taste of Nepal From Momos to Dal Bhat &rarr;</a> is a useful starting point.</p>

    <h2>Assuming Every Journey Will Be Quick</h2>

    <p>Distances can be misleading in Nepal.</p>

    <p>Road conditions, mountain terrain, traffic and weather can all affect how long a journey takes.</p>

    <p>If you have an important flight or activity later in the day, avoid creating a schedule that depends on everything happening perfectly.</p>

    <h2>Forgetting to Leave Buffer Time</h2>

    <p>Travel days rarely go exactly according to plan.</p>

    <p>A delayed vehicle or changed schedule can have a knock-on effect if your itinerary has no spare time.</p>

    <p>A little flexibility can protect the rest of your trip from one unexpected delay.</p>

    <h2>Staying Only in Tourist Areas</h2>

    <p>Tourist areas can be convenient, particularly when you are visiting for the first time.</p>

    <p>But spending all your time there can make Nepal feel more generic than it actually is.</p>

    <p>Explore local markets, smaller restaurants and neighborhoods when practical. You do not need to avoid tourist areas; simply balance them with everyday experiences.</p>

    <h2>Making Every Day an Early Start</h2>

    <p>Sunrise viewpoints and early departures can be worth it, but doing this every morning can make a longer trip exhausting.</p>

    <p>Mix demanding days with slower ones.</p>

    <p>A relaxed breakfast and an afternoon exploring Kathmandu can be just as valuable as a packed sightseeing schedule.</p>

    <h2>Ignoring Your Travel Style</h2>

    <p>There is no single correct way to experience Nepal.</p>

    <p>If you enjoy hiking, build your trip around trails. If you prefer culture and food, spend more time in cities and towns. If you want scenery without strenuous activity, focus on accessible viewpoints and destinations such as Pokhara.</p>

    <p>The best itinerary is the one that fits the way you actually like to travel.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What is the biggest mistake first-time visitors make in Nepal?</summary>
      <p>Trying to fit too many destinations into a short trip is a common planning problem. Mountain geography and travel conditions can make journeys take longer than expected.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Do I need to trek to enjoy Nepal?</summary>
      <p>No. Nepal also offers historic cities, food, markets, lakes, cultural experiences and mountain scenery that can be enjoyed without a major trek.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What should I pack for Nepal?</summary>
      <p>It depends on your route and season, but layers, comfortable walking shoes and sun protection are useful starting points. Trekking trips require more specialized equipment.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Should I leave extra time between destinations?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Allowing buffer time can make your itinerary more comfortable because road conditions, weather and other travel delays can affect journey times.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Nepal becomes easier to enjoy when you stop trying to plan it like a fast city break.</p>

    <p>Give yourself time to adjust to altitude, expect changing weather, leave room for slower journeys and explore beyond the main tourist route.</p>

    <p>Most importantly, build the trip around the kind of experience you actually want rather than the itinerary you think you are supposed to follow.</p>
  `
