  content: `
    <p>Fjords are probably the first thing most people associate with Norway.</p>

    <p>They deserve the attention. Deep blue water surrounded by steep mountains is one of the country's most recognizable landscapes.</p>

    <p>But if you build your entire Norway trip around fjords, you may miss how varied the country actually is.</p>

    <p>Norway stretches from cities and forests in the south to Arctic landscapes in the north. Between them are coastal communities, islands, mountain plateaus, waterfalls and roads where the scenery changes every few kilometers.</p>

    <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
      The fjords may be Norway's signature landscape, but they are not the whole Norwegian story.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Start With the Cities</h2>

    <p>Oslo and Bergen offer very different introductions to Norway.</p>

    <p>Oslo combines museums, parks, modern architecture, restaurants and waterfront areas. It is useful for travelers who want a city experience before heading into nature.</p>

    <p>Bergen, meanwhile, is closely connected with western Norway's mountains and fjords. Its historic harbor and surrounding hills make the city itself part of the scenic experience.</p>

    <p>Spending time in a Norwegian city before heading into the countryside can also give you a useful contrast. The quiet landscapes feel even more dramatic after the energy of the city.</p>

    <h2>The Coast Is More Than Fjords</h2>

    <p>Norway's coastline extends far beyond the famous fjord regions.</p>

    <p>Fishing villages, islands and open sea create a completely different kind of landscape. In northern areas, mountains can rise directly from the coast, while smaller communities sit between water and rock.</p>

    <p>This is one reason coastal road trips can be so rewarding. You are constantly moving between different versions of the same country.</p>

    <p>For a closer look at the north, read <a href="/blog/what-makes-northern-norway-feel-so-remote" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">What Makes Northern Norway Feel So Remote &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <h2>Norway's Mountains Stand on Their Own</h2>

    <p>You do not have to be standing beside a fjord to experience Norway's mountains.</p>

    <p>Mountain plateaus, valleys and hiking areas create opportunities for travelers who prefer walking and outdoor experiences. Some routes are challenging, while others are suitable for shorter scenic walks.</p>

    <p>The important thing is to choose activities based on your experience and current conditions rather than assuming every famous viewpoint is an easy walk.</p>

    <h2>The Forests and Lakes Feel Different Again</h2>

    <p>Southern and central Norway also offer landscapes that receive less attention internationally.</p>

    <p>Forests, lakes and rolling countryside create a quieter atmosphere than the dramatic western fjords. These areas can be particularly appealing for travelers who want nature without constantly moving between major viewpoints.</p>

    <p>You can spend a day hiking, cycling, kayaking or simply exploring small communities nearby.</p>

    <h2>Roads Become Part of the Experience</h2>

    <p>Norway's scenic roads are often attractions in their own right.</p>

    <p>Mountain passes, coastal routes and roads beside lakes and fjords can turn a simple transfer into one of the memorable parts of a trip.</p>

    <p>But scenic driving requires realistic planning. Weather, road conditions, ferries and seasonal closures can affect travel time.</p>

    <p>If you prefer not to spend every day behind the wheel, there are other ways to combine cities and nature. <a href="/blog/how-to-travel-norway-without-spending-every-day-driving" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">How to Travel Norway Without Spending Every Day Driving &rarr;</a> explores a slower approach.</p>

    <h2>Small Towns Add Another Layer</h2>

    <p>Some of Norway's character appears in places that are not famous internationally.</p>

    <p>Small coastal towns, mountain communities and villages can make useful stops between major destinations. They give you a chance to experience local cafés, harbors, wooden buildings and everyday life.</p>

    <p>Staying overnight rather than simply driving through can make these places feel much more meaningful.</p>

    <p>Read <a href="/blog/small-towns-where-norway-slows-down" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Small Towns Where Norway Slows Down &rarr;</a> for ideas on building quieter stops into your route.</p>

    <h2>The North Changes the Scale Completely</h2>

    <p>Northern Norway offers another perspective.</p>

    <p>The farther north you travel, the more the combination of distance, Arctic light and open landscapes begins to shape the experience.</p>

    <p>Depending on the season, you can experience long daylight hours or long winter nights. The northern lights are another reason travelers head north, but the landscape itself is worth the journey regardless of whether you see them.</p>

    <h2>Norway Is Better When You Mix Experiences</h2>

    <p>A memorable Norway itinerary does not need to be a sequence of fjord viewpoints.</p>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>Try Mixing These Experiences</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>City:</strong> museums, architecture and local food.</li>
        <li><strong>Coast:</strong> fishing villages and open sea.</li>
        <li><strong>Mountains:</strong> hiking and scenic viewpoints.</li>
        <li><strong>Small towns:</strong> slower days and local experiences.</li>
        <li><strong>Arctic north:</strong> dramatic landscapes and seasonal light.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>This creates more contrast and prevents the trip from feeling repetitive.</p>

    <h2>Leave Room for the Landscape</h2>

    <p>Norway rewards travelers who do not over-plan every hour.</p>

    <p>Give yourself time to stop at a viewpoint, explore a village or take a longer lunch. A scenic country like Norway does not always need another attraction added to the itinerary.</p>

    <p>Sometimes the road itself is enough.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Are Norway's fjords worth visiting?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Fjords are one of Norway's defining landscapes, but they are best experienced as part of a wider trip that includes other regions and environments.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What can I see in Norway besides fjords?</summary>
      <p>You can explore cities, forests, mountain areas, coastal villages, Arctic landscapes, islands and scenic roads.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Do I need to rent a car in Norway?</summary>
      <p>No. Trains, buses, ferries, flights and organized tours can connect many popular destinations, although a car provides additional flexibility in some regions.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>How can I see different parts of Norway on one trip?</summary>
      <p>Choose a route that combines a city with one or two natural regions rather than trying to cover the entire country. This keeps travel time manageable.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Norway's fjords are famous for a reason, but they are only the beginning.</p>

    <p>The country's cities, mountains, forests, coastlines, Arctic landscapes and small communities create a much broader travel experience.</p>

    <p>So when planning Norway, do not simply ask which fjord you should visit. Ask what kind of journey you want. A little variety can turn a beautiful trip into a much more memorable one.</p>
  `
