  content: `
    <p>Milan is usually introduced through two things: fashion and the Duomo.</p>

    <p>Both are important, but they only show part of the city.</p>

    <p>Spend a little more time in Milan and you start to notice a different side. Historic courtyards sit beside modern design studios, neighborhood cafés stay busy throughout the day and aperitivo turns the evening into its own social ritual.</p>

    <p>Milan is a city that becomes more interesting when you stop treating it as a quick stop between other Italian destinations.</p>

    <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
      Milan is not only a city of famous landmarks. Much of its character is found in the streets between them.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Start With the Duomo, Then Keep Walking</h2>

    <p>The Duomo is an obvious starting point.</p>

    <p>Its scale, architecture and position in the heart of Milan make it one of Italy's most recognizable landmarks. The surrounding Piazza del Duomo and Galleria Vittorio Emanuele II are also worth seeing.</p>

    <p>But once you have explored the centre, resist the temptation to leave Milan immediately.</p>

    <p>Walk outward and the atmosphere begins to change. The crowds thin, residential streets appear and smaller cafés and shops become part of the scenery.</p>

    <h2>Brera Brings Out Milan's Artistic Side</h2>

    <p>Brera is one of the city's best-known neighborhoods and a natural place to explore if you want something beyond the major landmarks.</p>

    <p>Its narrow streets, boutiques, galleries and cafés give the area a distinctly artistic atmosphere.</p>

    <p>The Pinacoteca di Brera is another reason to spend time here if you are interested in Italian art.</p>

    <p>Rather than rushing through the neighborhood, leave time for a slow walk and a coffee.</p>

    <h2>Navigli Feels Different After Sunset</h2>

    <p>The Navigli area is built around Milan's historic canals and becomes especially lively in the evening.</p>

    <p>This is one of the places where you can see a more social side of the city.</p>

    <p>Restaurants, bars and cafés line the canals, while people gather for aperitivo and evening conversations.</p>

    <p>You do not need a complicated plan. Arrive before sunset, walk along the canals and stay for dinner or drinks.</p>

    <h2>Milan's Aperitivo Culture</h2>

    <p>Aperitivo is more than simply having a drink before dinner.</p>

    <p>It is part of the city's evening rhythm, with people meeting friends over drinks and small plates before moving on to dinner.</p>

    <p>For visitors, it can be a relaxed way to experience Milan without planning another major attraction.</p>

    <p>It is also a useful reminder that experiencing a city does not always mean sightseeing.</p>

    <h2>Don't Ignore Milanese Food</h2>

    <p>Milan has its own regional food traditions, and they are different from the dishes many travelers associate with southern Italy.</p>

    <p>Risotto alla Milanese, for example, is known for its distinctive saffron color and rich flavor. Ossobuco is another classic Milanese dish, often served alongside risotto or other traditional accompaniments.</p>

    <p>Panettone is also closely associated with Milan and is now famous far beyond Italy.</p>

    <p>If you want to explore how Italian cuisine changes from region to region, read <a href="/blog/northern-italy-and-southern-italy-feel-like-different-trips" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Northern Italy and Southern Italy Feel Like Different Trips &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <div class="food-card">
      <h3>Milanese Foods to Look For</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Risotto alla Milanese:</strong> saffron-infused creamy risotto.</li>
        <li><strong>Ossobuco:</strong> slow-cooked veal shank traditionally served with rich accompaniments.</li>
        <li><strong>Cotoletta:</strong> a Milanese-style breaded veal cutlet.</li>
        <li><strong>Panettone:</strong> the famous Milanese sweet bread.</li>
        <li><strong>Aperitivo:</strong> an evening tradition combining drinks and small bites.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Design Is Everywhere</h2>

    <p>Milan's reputation as a design capital extends beyond fashion.</p>

    <p>Architecture, furniture, interiors and contemporary design are all part of the city's identity.</p>

    <p>Even if you are not visiting during a major design event, you can notice this influence in shops, showrooms, galleries and modern buildings throughout the city.</p>

    <p>It is one reason Milan can feel noticeably different from cities such as Rome or Florence.</p>

    <h2>Explore a Neighborhood Without a Checklist</h2>

    <p>One of the best ways to experience Milan is to choose a neighborhood and spend a few hours there without trying to see everything.</p>

    <p>Find a café, walk through side streets, visit a local shop and stop whenever something catches your attention.</p>

    <p>This slower approach can reveal details that are easy to miss when you move from one famous attraction to another.</p>

    <h2>Give Yourself More Than One Day</h2>

    <p>Milan can technically be visited as a short stop, but giving it two or three days creates a much better balance.</p>

    <p>One day can focus on the historic centre, another on neighborhoods such as Brera and Navigli, and another on museums, shopping or a nearby attraction.</p>

    <p>If Milan is part of a longer Italy trip, you can also use it as a gateway toward northern destinations and the Italian lakes.</p>

    <p>For inspiration, see <a href="/blog/the-italian-lakes-that-deserve-more-than-a-day-trip" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">The Italian Lakes That Deserve More Than a Day Trip &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <h2>A Simple Milan Day</h2>

    <div class="itinerary-card">
      <h3>A Relaxed Day in Milan</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Morning:</strong> Duomo and the historic centre.</li>
        <li><strong>Late morning:</strong> Galleria Vittorio Emanuele II and nearby streets.</li>
        <li><strong>Afternoon:</strong> Brera, galleries and a long café stop.</li>
        <li><strong>Evening:</strong> Navigli and aperitivo.</li>
        <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Try a traditional Milanese dish.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>You can adjust this depending on your interests. The important part is leaving enough time between attractions rather than rushing from one side of the city to another.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is Milan worth visiting beyond the Duomo?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Brera, Navigli, Milanese food, design, museums and neighborhood life offer plenty to experience beyond the city's most famous landmark.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>How many days do you need in Milan?</summary>
      <p>Two or three days can give you time to see the major sights while also exploring neighborhoods, food and local experiences at a slower pace.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What food is Milan famous for?</summary>
      <p>Risotto alla Milanese, ossobuco, cotoletta and panettone are among the city's best-known traditional foods.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>What is the best area to experience Milan at night?</summary>
      <p>Navigli is particularly well known for its evening atmosphere, restaurants, bars and canals, while other neighborhoods offer their own nightlife and dining scenes.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Milan deserves more than a quick photograph of the Duomo.</p>

    <p>Its neighborhoods, food, design culture and evening atmosphere reveal a city that feels very different once you slow down.</p>

    <p>See the famous sights, but leave enough time to wander. In Milan, some of the best parts of the trip can happen after you leave the main square.</p>
  `
