  content: `
    <p>Buenos Aires is often the first image people have of Argentina. Elegant boulevards, tango, cafés, football and late-night restaurants all make the capital an exciting place to begin. But Argentina is so large that stopping there would mean seeing only a small part of the country.</p>

    <p>Travel farther and the landscape changes dramatically. Vineyards meet the Andes around Mendoza, enormous glaciers shape Patagonia, tropical forests surround Iguazú Falls and wide-open plains stretch across the country.</p>

    <blockquote class="editorial-quote">Argentina is not one travel experience. Its regions can feel like completely different journeys within the same country.</blockquote>

    <h2>Buenos Aires Is Only the Beginning</h2>

    <p>Buenos Aires deserves time. The neighborhoods of Palermo, San Telmo and Recoleta each offer a different side of the city, while its cafés and restaurants make it easy to spend an entire afternoon without a strict sightseeing plan.</p>

    <p>But after several days, Argentina's scale starts to become more apparent. This is when adding another region can completely change the trip.</p>

    <h2>Mendoza Brings the Andes Into the Picture</h2>

    <p>West of Buenos Aires, Mendoza offers a very different experience. The region is famous for vineyards, but wine is only part of the attraction.</p>

    <p>The Andes provide a dramatic backdrop, and outdoor activities can range from relaxed vineyard visits to mountain adventures. Mendoza works particularly well for travelers who want food, scenery and outdoor experiences in the same trip.</p>

    <h2>Patagonia Feels Like Another World</h2>

    <p>Further south, Patagonia introduces some of Argentina's most dramatic landscapes. Mountains, lakes, glaciers and vast open spaces dominate the region.</p>

    <p>El Calafate is an important base for exploring the southern part of Patagonia, while El Chaltén is known for hiking routes and mountain scenery.</p>

    <p>Patagonia requires more planning than a Buenos Aires city break, but the reward is a completely different side of Argentina.</p>

    <p>For a deeper look at the region, read our <a href="/blog/patagonia-beyond-the-famous-landscapes" style="color: #2563eb; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: 700;">Patagonia travel guide &rarr;</a>.</p>

    <div class="destination-card">
      <h3>Argentina by Region</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buenos Aires:</strong> Culture, architecture, food and nightlife</li>
        <li><strong>Mendoza:</strong> Vineyards, Andes scenery and outdoor experiences</li>
        <li><strong>Patagonia:</strong> Glaciers, mountains, lakes and hiking</li>
        <li><strong>Iguazú:</strong> Tropical forest and massive waterfalls</li>
        <li><strong>Northwest:</strong> High-altitude landscapes, colorful mountains and historic towns</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Iguazú Shows a Tropical Side of Argentina</h2>

    <p>At the country's northeastern edge, Iguazú Falls creates another dramatic contrast. Instead of Patagonia's cold landscapes, the region is surrounded by subtropical forest and warm, humid conditions.</p>

    <p>The waterfalls can be experienced from different viewpoints and walking routes, making the area very different from both Buenos Aires and southern Argentina.</p>

    <h2>The Northwest Has Its Own Identity</h2>

    <p>Argentina's northwest is another region worth considering. Around Salta and Jujuy, the scenery changes to high-altitude valleys, colorful rock formations, traditional towns and mountain roads.</p>

    <p>The region also has strong cultural and culinary traditions that differ from the cosmopolitan character of Buenos Aires.</p>

    <h2>How to Choose Your Argentina Route</h2>

    <p>The biggest mistake when planning Argentina is treating it like a small European country where you can casually move between destinations every few hours.</p>

    <p>Distances are enormous. Instead of trying to see everything, choose regions that match the type of trip you want.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>City and culture:</strong> Buenos Aires</li>
      <li><strong>Wine and mountains:</strong> Mendoza</li>
      <li><strong>Hiking and dramatic landscapes:</strong> Patagonia</li>
      <li><strong>Waterfalls and nature:</strong> Iguazú</li>
      <li><strong>Culture and high-altitude scenery:</strong> Northwest Argentina</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Long Should You Stay?</h2>

    <p>A week is enough for a focused regional trip, but a longer visit makes it easier to combine Buenos Aires with another part of the country.</p>

    <p>For example, Buenos Aires plus Mendoza creates a very different trip from Buenos Aires plus Patagonia. Adding too many regions can result in spending more time in airports than actually experiencing Argentina.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is Argentina worth visiting beyond Buenos Aires?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Argentina has major regional differences, including Patagonia, Mendoza, Iguazú Falls and the mountainous northwest.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>How many days do you need in Argentina?</summary>
      <p>A week can work for one or two regions, while longer trips allow you to combine Buenos Aires with destinations such as Patagonia or Mendoza.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-accordion">
      <summary>Is Argentina difficult to travel around?</summary>
      <p>The country is manageable for travelers, but its enormous distances mean that flights are often useful when connecting far-apart regions.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

    <p>Buenos Aires may introduce you to Argentina, but it does not define the country. From Mendoza's vineyards and the Andes to Patagonia's glaciers and Iguazú's tropical forests, Argentina changes dramatically as you travel.</p>

    <p>The best approach is not to see everything. Choose the regions that match your interests and give them enough time to actually experience them.</p>
  `
